From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Blechmann <tim.blechmann@qt.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] AlignedAllocator: override allocate_at_least

std::allocator provides an allocate_at_least() member since C++23
(libc++ exposes it unconditionally). std::allocator_traits prefers that
member over calling allocate(), so AlignedAllocator inherited it and
containers obtained memory from plain operator new. deallocate() then
passed that pointer to AllignedFree(), which reads a saved malloc pointer
8 bytes before the block. This is a heap-buffer-overflow read that ASan
reports, e.g. from AudioBuffer destruction in CreateShHrirsFromWav().

Override allocate_at_least() to route through the aligned allocate().

---
 resonance_audio/base/aligned_allocator.h | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/resonance_audio/base/aligned_allocator.h b/resonance_audio/base/aligned_allocator.h
index 63c1ee2f8..21c545ea7 100644
--- a/resonance_audio/base/aligned_allocator.h
+++ b/resonance_audio/base/aligned_allocator.h
@@ -95,9 +95,12 @@ class AlignedAllocator : public std::allocator<Type> {
     return AllignedMalloc<Type, SizeType, Pointer>(size, Alignment);
   }
 
+#if defined(__cpp_lib_allocate_at_least)
+  // Only exists where std::allocator provides allocate_at_least() (C++23).
   std::allocation_result<Pointer, SizeType> allocate_at_least(SizeType size) {
     return {allocate(size), size};
   }
+#endif
 
   void deallocate(Pointer mem_block_aligned, size_t size) {
     AllignedFree<Pointer>(mem_block_aligned);
-- 
2.39.5

